## sn35.127
sn35.127:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Bhāradvāja the Alms-Gatherer was staying near Kosambī in Ghosita’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Venerable Piṇḍola Bharadvaja was dwelling at Kosambī in Ghosita’s Park.
sn35.127:1.2 | S: Then King Udena went up to Bhāradvāja the Alms-Gatherer and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then King Udena approached the Venerable Piṇḍola Bharadvaja and exchanged greetings with him.
sn35.127:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: When they had concluded their greetings and cordial talk, he sat down to one side and said to him:
sn35.127:1.4 | S: “Worthy Bhāradvāja, there are these young monks who are youthful, with pristine black hair, blessed with youth, in the prime of life; and they’ve never played around with sensual pleasures. What is the cause, what is the reason why they practice the full and pure spiritual life as long as they live, maintaining it for a long time?” | T: “Master Bharadvaja, what is the cause and reason why these young bhikkhus, lads with black hair, endowed with the blessing of youth, in the prime of life, who have not dallied with sensual pleasures, lead the complete and pure holy life all their lives and maintain it continuously?”
sn35.127:1.5 | S: “Great king, this has been stated by the Blessed One, who knows and sees, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha: | T: “Great king, this was said by the Blessed One who knows and sees, the Arahant, the Fully Enlightened One:
sn35.127:1.6 | S: ‘Please, monks, think of women your mother’s age as your mother. Think of women your sister’s age as your sister. And think of women your daughter’s age as your daughter.’ | T: ‘Come, bhikkhus, towards women old enough to be your mother set up the idea that they are your mother; towards those of an age to be your sisters set up the idea that they are your sisters; towards those young enough to be your daughters set up the idea that they are your daughters.’
sn35.127:1.7 | S: This is a cause, Great King, this is a reason why these young monks practice the full and pure spiritual life as long as they live, maintaining it for a long time.” | T: This is a cause and reason, great king, why these young bhikkhus … lead the complete and pure holy life all their lives and maintain it continuously.”
sn35.127:2.1 | S: “But worthy Bhāradvāja, the mind is wanton. | T: “The mind is wanton, Master Bharadvaja.
sn35.127:2.2 | S: Sometimes thoughts of desire come up even for women your mother’s age, your sister’s age, or your daughter’s age. | T: Sometimes states of lust arise even towards women old enough to be one’s mother; sometimes they arise towards women of an age to be one’s sister; sometimes they arise towards women young enough to be one’s daughter.
sn35.127:2.3 | S: Is there another cause, another reason why these young monks live the full and pure spiritual life for their entire life?” | T: Is there any other cause and reason why these young bhikkhus … lead the complete and pure holy life all their lives and maintain it continuously?”
sn35.127:3.1 | S: “Great king, this has been stated by the Blessed One, who knows and sees, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha: | T: “Great king, this was said by the Blessed One who knows and sees, the Arahant, the Fully Enlightened One:
sn35.127:3.2 | S: ‘Please, monks, examine your own body up from the soles of the feet and down from the tips of the hairs, wrapped in skin and full of many kinds of filth. | T: ‘Come, bhikkhus, review this very body upwards from the soles of the feet, downwards from the tips of the hairs, enclosed in skin, as full of many kinds of impurities:
sn35.127:3.3 | S: In this body there is head hair, body hair, nails, teeth, skin, flesh, sinews, bones, bone marrow, kidneys, heart, liver, diaphragm, spleen, lungs, intestines, mesentery, undigested food, feces, bile, phlegm, pus, blood, sweat, fat, tears, grease, saliva, snot, synovial fluid, urine.’ | T: “There are in this body head-hairs, body-hairs, nails, teeth, skin, flesh, sinews, bones, bone-marrow, kidneys, heart, liver, pleura, spleen, lungs, intestines, mesentery, contents of the stomach, excrement, bile, phlegm, pus, blood, sweat, fat, tears, grease, saliva, snot, fluid of the joints, urine.”’
sn35.127:3.4 | S: This is also a cause, Great King, this is a reason why these young monks live the full and pure spiritual life for their entire life, maintaining it for a long time.” | T: This too, great king, is a cause and reason why these young bhikkhus … lead the complete and pure holy life all their lives and maintain it continuously.”
sn35.127:3.5 | S: “This is easy to do for those mendicants who have developed their physical endurance, ethics, mind, and wisdom. | T: “That is easy, Master Bharadvaja, for those bhikkhus who are developed in body, developed in virtue, developed in mind, developed in wisdom.
sn35.127:3.6 | S: But it’s hard to do for those mendicants who have not developed their physical endurance, ethics, mind, and wisdom. | T: But it is difficult for those bhikkhus who are undeveloped in body, undeveloped in virtue, undeveloped in mind, undeveloped in wisdom.
sn35.127:3.7 | S: Sometimes I plan to focus on something as ugly, but only its beauty comes to mind. | T: Sometimes, though one thinks, ‘I will attend to the body as foul,’ one beholds it as beautiful.
sn35.127:3.8 | S: Is there another cause, another reason why these young monks live the full and pure spiritual life for their entire life?” | T: Is there any other cause and reason why these young bhikkhus … lead the complete and pure holy life all their lives and maintain it continuously?”
sn35.127:4.1 | S: “Great king, this has been stated by the Blessed One, who knows and sees, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha: | T: “Great king, this was said by the Blessed One who knows and sees, the Arahant, the Fully Enlightened One:
sn35.127:4.2 | S: ‘Please, monks, live with sense doors guarded. | T: ‘Come, bhikkhus, dwell guarding the doors of the sense faculties.
sn35.127:4.3 | S: When you see a sight with your eyes, don’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: Having seen a form with the eye, do not grasp its signs and features.
sn35.127:4.4 | S: If the faculty of sight were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, practice restraint, protect the faculty of sight, and achieve its restraint. | T: Since, if you leave the eye faculty unguarded, evil unwholesome states of covetousness and displeasure might invade you, practise the way of its restraint, guard the eye faculty, undertake the restraint of the eye faculty.
sn35.127:4.5 | S: When you hear a sound with your ears … | T: Having heard a sound with the ear…
sn35.127:4.6 | S: When you smell an odor with your nose … | T: Having smelt an odour with the nose …
sn35.127:4.7 | S: When you taste a flavor with your tongue … | T: Having savoured a taste with the tongue …
sn35.127:4.8 | S: When you feel a touch with your body … | T: Having felt a tactile object with the body …
sn35.127:4.9 | S: When you know an idea with your mind, don’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: Having cognized a mental phenomenon with the mind, do not grasp its signs and features.
sn35.127:4.10 | S: If the faculty of mind were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, practice restraint, protect the faculty of mind, and achieve its restraint.’ | T: Since, if you leave the mind faculty unguarded, evil unwholesome states of covetousness and displeasure might invade you, practise the way of its restraint, guard the mind faculty, undertake the restraint of the mind faculty.’
sn35.127:4.11 | S: This is also a cause, Great King, this is a reason why these young monks practice the full and pure spiritual life as long as they live, maintaining it for a long time.” | T: This too, great king, is a cause and reason why these young bhikkhus … lead the complete and pure holy life all their lives and maintain it continuously.”
sn35.127:5.1 | S: “It’s incredible, worthy Bhāradvāja, it’s amazing! | T: “It is wonderful, Master Bharadvaja! It is amazing, Master Bharadvaja!
sn35.127:5.2 | S: How well this was said by the Buddha! | T: How well this has been stated by the Blessed One who knows and sees, the Arahant, the Fully Enlightened One.
sn35.127:5.3 | S: This is the real cause, this is the reason why these young monks practice the full and pure spiritual life as long as they live, maintaining it for a long time. | T: So this is the cause and reason why these young bhikkhus, lads with black hair, endowed with the blessing of youth, in the prime of life, who have not dallied with sensual pleasures, lead the complete and pure holy life all their lives and maintain it continuously.
sn35.127:5.4 | S: For sometimes I too enter the harem with unprotected body, speech, mind, mindfulness, and sense faculties. At those times powerful thoughts of desire get the better of me. | T: In my case too, when I enter my harem unguarded in body, speech, and mind, without setting up mindfulness, unrestrained in the sense faculties, on that occasion states of lust assail me forcefully.
sn35.127:5.5 | S: But sometimes I enter the harem with protected body, speech, mind, mindfulness, and sense faculties. At those times such thoughts of desire don’t get the better of me. | T: But when I enter my harem guarded in body, speech, and mind, with mindfulness set up, restrained in the sense faculties, on that occasion states of lust do not assail me in such a way.
sn35.127:5.6 | S: Excellent, worthy Bhāradvāja! Excellent! | T: “Magnificent, Master Bharadvaja! Magnificent, Master Bharadvaja!
sn35.127:5.7 | S: As if he were righting the overturned, or revealing the hidden, or pointing out the path to the lost, or lighting a lamp in the dark so people with clear eyes can see what’s there, the worthy Bhāradvāja has made the teaching clear in many ways. | T: The Dhamma has been made clear in many ways by Master Bharadvaja, as though he were turning upright what had been turned upside down, revealing what was hidden, showing the way to one who was lost, or holding up a lamp in the dark for those with eyesight to see forms.
sn35.127:5.8 | S: I go for refuge to the Buddha, to the teaching, and to the mendicant Saṅgha. | T: Master Bharadvaja, I go for refuge to the Blessed One, and to the Dhamma, and to the Bhikkhu Saṅgha.
sn35.127:5.9 | S: From this day forth, may the worthy Bhāradvāja remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.” | T: From today let Master Bharadvaja remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.”
sn35.127:5.10 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.129
sn35.129:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Ānanda was staying near Kosambī in Ghosita’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Venerable Ānanda was dwelling at Kosambī in Ghosita’s Park.
sn35.129:1.2 | S: Then the householder Ghosita went up to Venerable Ānanda, and said to him: | T: Then the householder Ghosita approached the Venerable Ānanda … and said to him:
sn35.129:1.3 | S: “Honorable Ānanda, they speak of ‘the diversity of elements’. | T: “Venerable Ānanda, it is said, ‘diversity of elements, diversity of elements.’
sn35.129:1.4 | S: In what way did the Buddha speak of the diversity of elements?” | T: In what way, venerable sir, has the diversity of elements been spoken of by the Blessed One?”
sn35.129:1.5 | S: “Householder, the eye element is found, as are agreeable sights, and eye consciousness. | T: “Householder, there exists the eye element, and forms that are agreeable, and eye-consciousness:
sn35.129:1.6 | S: Pleasant feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as pleasant. | T: in dependence on a contact to be experienced as pleasant, a pleasant feeling arises.
sn35.129:1.7 | S: The eye element is found, as are disagreeable sights, and eye consciousness. | T: There exists the eye element, and forms that are disagreeable, and eye-consciousness:
sn35.129:1.8 | S: Painful feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as painful. | T: in dependence on a contact to be experienced as painful, a painful feeling arises.
sn35.129:1.9 | S: The eye element is found, as are sights that are a basis for equanimity, and eye consciousness. | T: There exists the eye element, and forms that are a basis for equanimity, and eye-consciousness:
sn35.129:1.10 | S: Neutral feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as neutral. | T: in dependence on a contact to be experienced as neither-painful-nor-pleasant, a neither- painful-nor-pleasant feeling arises.
sn35.129:1.11 | S: The ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: “Householder, there exists the ear element … the nose element … the tongue element … the body element …
sn35.129:1.12 | P: Phassaṁ paṭicca uppajjati sukhā vedanā. | T: (none)
sn35.129:1.13 | P: Saṁvijjati kho, gahapati, jivhādhātu, rasā ca amanāpā, jivhāviññāṇañca dukkhavedaniyaṁ. | T: (none)
sn35.129:1.14 | P: Phassaṁ paṭicca uppajjati dukkhā vedanā. | T: (none)
sn35.129:1.15 | P: Saṁvijjati kho, gahapati, jivhādhātu, rasā ca upekkhāvedaniyā, jivhāviññāṇañca adukkhamasukhavedaniyaṁ. | T: (none)
sn35.129:1.16 | P: Phassaṁ paṭicca uppajjati adukkhamasukhā vedanā …pe… | T: (none)
sn35.129:1.17 | S: mind element is found, as are agreeable ideas, and mind consciousness. | T: the mind element, and mental phenomena that are agreeable, and mind-consciousness:
sn35.129:1.18 | S: Pleasant feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as pleasant. | T: in dependence on a contact to be experienced as pleasant, a pleasant feeling arises.
sn35.129:1.19 | S: The mind element is found, as are disagreeable ideas, and mind consciousness. | T: There exists the mind element, and mental phenomena that are disagreeable, and mind-consciousness:
sn35.129:1.20 | S: Painful feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as painful. | T: in dependence on a contact to be experienced as painful, a painful feeling arises.
sn35.129:1.21 | S: The mind element is found, as are ideas that are a basis for equanimity, and mind consciousness. | T: There exists the mind element, and mental phenomena that are a basis for equanimity, and mind-consciousness:
sn35.129:1.22 | S: Neutral feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as neutral. | T: in dependence on a contact to be experienced as neither-painful-nor-pleasant, a neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling arises.
sn35.129:1.23 | S: This is how the Buddha spoke of the diversity of elements.” | T: “It is in this way, householder, that the diversity of elements has been spoken of by the Blessed One.”
sn35.129:1.24 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.134
sn35.134:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Sakyans, near the Sakyan town named Devadaha. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling among the Sakyans where there was a town of the Sakyans named Devadaha.
sn35.134:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants: | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn35.134:1.3 | S: “When it comes to the six fields of contact, mendicants, I don’t say that all mendicants have work to do with diligence, nor do I say that none of them have work to do with diligence. | T: “Bhikkhus, I do not say of all bhikkhus that they still have work to do with diligence in regard to the six bases for contact, nor do I say of all bhikkhus that they do not have work to do with diligence in regard to the six bases for contact.
sn35.134:1.4 | S: I say that, when it comes to the six fields of contact, mendicants don’t have work to do with diligence if they are perfected, with defilements ended, having completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their heart’s goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and become rightly freed through enlightenment. | T: “I do not say of those bhikkhus who are arahants, whose taints are destroyed, who have lived the holy life, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, reached their own goal, utterly destroyed the fetters of existence, and are completely liberated through final knowledge, that they still have work to do with diligence in regard to the six bases for contact.
sn35.134:1.5 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn35.134:1.6 | S: They’ve done their work with diligence, and are incapable of negligence. | T: They have done their work with diligence; they are incapable of being negligent.
sn35.134:1.7 | S: I say that, when it comes to the six fields of contact, mendicants do have work to do with diligence if they are trainees, who haven’t achieved their heart’s desire, but live aspiring to the supreme sanctuary from the yoke. | T: “But I say of those bhikkhus who are trainees, who have not attained their mind’s ideal, who dwell aspiring for the unsurpassed security from bondage, that they still have work to do with diligence in regard to the six bases for contact.
sn35.134:1.8 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn35.134:1.9 | S: There are sights known by the eye that are pleasant and also those that are unpleasant. | T: There are, bhikkhus, forms cognizable by the eye that are agreeable and those that are disagreeable.
sn35.134:1.10 | S: Though experiencing them again and again they don’t occupy the mind. | T: One should train so that these do not persist obsessing one’s mind even when they are repeatedly experienced.
sn35.134:1.11 | S: Their energy is roused up and unflagging, their mindfulness is established and lucid, their body is tranquil and undisturbed, and their mind is immersed and unified in samādhi. | T: When the mind is not obsessed, tireless energy is aroused, unmuddled mindfulness is set up, the body becomes tranquil and untroubled, the mind becomes concentrated and one-pointed.
sn35.134:1.12 | S: Seeing this fruit of diligence, I say that those mendicants have work to do with diligence when it comes to the six fields of contact. … | T: Seeing this fruit of diligence, bhikkhus, I say that those bhikkhus still have work to do with diligence in regard to the six bases for contact.
sn35.134:1.13 | S: There are ideas known by the mind that are pleasant and also those that are unpleasant. | T: “There are, bhikkhus, sounds cognizable by the ear … mental phenomena cognizable by the mind that are agreeable and those that are disagreeable.
sn35.134:1.14 | S: Though experiencing them again and again they don’t occupy the mind. | T: One should train so that these do not persist obsessing one’s mind even when they are repeatedly experienced.
sn35.134:1.15 | S: Their energy is roused up and unflagging, their mindfulness is established and lucid, their body is tranquil and undisturbed, and their mind is immersed and unified in samādhi. | T: When the mind is not obsessed, tireless energy is aroused, unmuddled mindfulness is set up, the body becomes tranquil and untroubled, the mind becomes concentrated and one-pointed.
sn35.134:1.16 | S: Seeing this fruit of diligence, I say that those mendicants have work to do with diligence when it comes to the six fields of contact.” | T: Seeing this fruit of diligence, bhikkhus, I say that those bhikkhus still have work to do with diligence in regard to the six bases for contact.”
sn35.134:1.17 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.135
