## sn35.147
sn35.147:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you a practice that’s conducive to extinguishment. | T: “Bhikkhus, I will teach you the way that is suitable for attaining Nibbāna.
sn35.147:1.2 | S: Listen … | T: Listen to that….
sn35.147:1.3 | S: And what is that practice that’s conducive to extinguishment? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is the way that is suitable for attaining Nibbāna?
sn35.147:1.4 | S: It’s when a mendicant sees that the eye, sights, eye consciousness, and eye contact are impermanent. And they see that the painful, pleasant, or neutral feeling that arises dependent on eye contact is also impermanent. | T: Here, a bhikkhu sees the eye as impermanent, he sees forms as impermanent, he sees eye-consciousness as impermanent, he sees eye-contact as impermanent, he sees as impermanent whatever feeling arises with eye-contact as condition, whether pleasant or painful or neither-painful-nor-pleasant.
sn35.147:1.5 | S: They see that the ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: “He sees the ear as impermanent … …
sn35.147:1.6 | S: mind, ideas, mind-consciousness, and mind contact are impermanent. And they see that the painful, pleasant, or neutral feeling that arises dependent on mind contact is also impermanent. | T: He sees the mind as impermanent, he sees mental phenomena as impermanent, he sees mind-consciousness as impermanent, he sees mind-contact as impermanent, he sees as impermanent whatever feeling arises with mind-contact as condition, whether pleasant or painful or neither-painful-nor-pleasant.
sn35.147:1.7 | S: This is that practice that’s conducive to extinguishment.” | T: “This, bhikkhus, is the way that is suitable for attaining Nibbāna.”
sn35.147:1.8 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.152
sn35.152:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, if wanderers who follow another religion were to ask you: | T: “Bhikkhus, if wanderers of other sects ask you:
