## sn35.1
sn35.1:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn35.1:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Savatthi in Jeta’s Grove, Anathapiṇḍika’s Park.
sn35.1:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn35.1:1.4 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Bhikkhus!”
sn35.1:1.5 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Venerable sir!” those bhikkhus replied.
sn35.1:1.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
sn35.1:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, the eye is impermanent. | T: “Bhikkhus, the eye is impermanent.
sn35.1:2.2 | S: What’s impermanent is suffering. | T: What is impermanent is suffering.
sn35.1:2.3 | S: What’s suffering is not-self. | T: What is suffering is nonself.
sn35.1:2.4 | S: And what’s not-self should be truly seen with right understanding like this: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: What is nonself should be seen as it really is with correct wisdom thus: ‘This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self.’
sn35.1:2.5 | S: The ear is impermanent. … | T: “The ear is impermanent….
sn35.1:2.6 | P: Yadaniccaṁ …pe… | T: (none)
sn35.1:2.7 | S: The nose is impermanent. … | T: The nose is impermanent….
sn35.1:2.8 | P: Yadaniccaṁ …pe… | T: (none)
sn35.1:2.9 | S: The tongue is impermanent. … | T: The tongue is impermanent….
sn35.1:2.10 | P: Yadaniccaṁ taṁ dukkhaṁ; | T: (none)
sn35.1:2.11 | P: yaṁ dukkhaṁ tadanattā. | T: (none)
sn35.1:2.12 | P: Yadanattā taṁ ‘netaṁ mama, nesohamasmi, na meso attā’ti evametaṁ yathābhūtaṁ sammappaññāya daṭṭhabbaṁ. | T: (none)
sn35.1:2.13 | S: The body is impermanent. … | T: The body is impermanent….
sn35.1:2.14 | P: Yadaniccaṁ …pe… | T: (none)
sn35.1:2.15 | S: The mind is impermanent. | T: The mind is impermanent.
sn35.1:2.16 | S: What’s impermanent is suffering. | T: What is impermanent is suffering.
sn35.1:2.17 | S: What’s suffering is not-self. | T: What is suffering is nonself.
sn35.1:2.18 | S: And what’s not-self should be truly seen with right understanding like this: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: What is nonself should be seen as it really is with correct wisdom thus: ‘This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self.’
sn35.1:2.19 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind. | T: “Seeing thus, bhikkhus, the instructed noble disciple experiences revulsion towards the eye, revulsion towards the ear, revulsion towards the nose, revulsion towards the tongue, revulsion towards the body, revulsion towards the mind.
sn35.1:2.20 | S: Being disillusioned, desire fades away. When desire fades away they’re freed. When they’re freed, they know they’re freed. | T: Experiencing revulsion, he becomes dispassionate. Through dispassion his mind is liberated. When it is liberated there comes the knowledge: ‘It’s liberated.’
sn35.1:2.21 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’” | T: He understands: ‘Destroyed is birth, the holy life has been lived, what had to be done has been done, there is no more for this state of being.’”
sn35.1:2.22 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)


